{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Additional corroborator safety correction (2026-09-03)\\n\\nTFLint must also be excluded from v1 execution without a strong filesystem sandbox. Current TFLint embeds Terraform's filesystem functions (file, fileexists, fileset, filebase64, file hashes, templatefile); its openFile canonicalizes absolute/relative/~ paths but does not confine them to the module/repository. Rebar-owned config, bundled-rules-only, and --call-module-type=none prevent repository plugin/module execution and fetching, but they do not prevent Terraform source from causing arbitrary readable host files to be opened during evaluation.\\n\\nHashiCorp terraform-config-inspect remains a viable optional PATH corroborator: its shallow loader uses HCL Expr.Value(nil), exposes only stable top-level module metadata, and does not register Terraform functions. V1 should use it only on a hardened repo snapshot, schema-check its JSON, capture its binary digest/version provenance, and abstain on errors/skew. Trivy remains deferred unless an invocation is proven both no-egress and filesystem-confined."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788463860116412001,"uuid":"20d7c7ab-4c40-400a-8915-5bdc69a2aa35"}